<h1>
                    Device and Input/Output Management
                </h1>
<p>
                    Learn how operating systems control
                    hardware devices, manage input and
                    output operations, coordinate data
                    transfers, and improve computer
                    performance.
                </p>
</header>
<!-- =====================================
                 BOOK CONTENT
            ====================================== -->
<div class="book-content">
<!-- =====================================
                     INTRODUCTION
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="introduction">
<h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>
                        Device and Input/Output Management
                        is an important function of an
                        operating system. It focuses on
                        controlling and coordinating
                        communication between the computer
                        and its connected hardware devices.
                    </p>
<p>
                        These devices include keyboards,
                        mice, monitors, printers, scanners,
                        speakers, microphones, storage drives,
                        and network adapters.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The operating system manages these
                        devices so users and applications
                        can interact with hardware without
                        needing to understand its internal
                        technical operations.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            When a student types using a
                            keyboard, the operating system
                            receives and processes the input.
                            When the student prints a document,
                            the operating system coordinates
                            with the printer to complete
                            the task.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     PART 1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="part1">
<span class="part-heading">
                        Part 1
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Fundamentals of Device and
                        Input/Output Management
                    </h2>
<p>
                        This part explains the basic concepts,
                        responsibilities, importance, and
                        objectives of device and I/O management.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 1.1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-1">
<h2>
                        1.1 What Is Device Management?
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Device management is the process by
                        which an operating system controls,
                        monitors, and coordinates hardware
                        devices connected to a computer.
                    </p>
<h3>Main Responsibilities</h3>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Device Control:</strong>
                            Controls hardware operations.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Device Communication:</strong>
                            Connects software with hardware.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Resource Allocation:</strong>
                            Assigns resources to devices.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Device Monitoring:</strong>
                            Checks device conditions.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Error Handling:</strong>
                            Detects and manages problems.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Device Coordination:</strong>
                            Organizes multiple devices.
                        </li>
</ol>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            When a USB flash drive is connected,
                            the operating system detects it,
                            identifies the device, loads a
                            suitable driver, and makes its
                            storage available.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 1.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-2">
<h2>
                        1.2 What Is Input/Output Management?
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Input/Output Management, or I/O
                        Management, is an operating system
                        function responsible for handling
                        information transfers between
                        computers and hardware devices.
                    </p>
<h3>Input Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Typing with a keyboard
                        </li>
<li>
                            Moving a mouse
                        </li>
<li>
                            Scanning documents
                        </li>
<li>
                            Recording audio through a microphone
                        </li>
<li>
                            Capturing images with a webcam
                        </li>
</ul>
<h3>Output Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Displaying information on a monitor
                        </li>
<li>
                            Printing documents
                        </li>
<li>
                            Playing sound through speakers
                        </li>
<li>
                            Displaying images with a projector
                        </li>
<li>
                            Sending information through
                            a network connection
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 1.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-3">
<h2>
                        1.3 Importance of Device and
                        I/O Management
                    </h2>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>
                                Efficient Communication:
                            </strong>
                            Allows hardware and software
                            to interact.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>
                                Better Performance:
                            </strong>
                            Reduces unnecessary delays.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>
                                Resource Sharing:
                            </strong>
                            Supports multiple applications
                            using devices.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>
                                Error Detection:
                            </strong>
                            Identifies hardware and
                            transfer problems.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>
                                User Convenience:
                            </strong>
                            Simplifies device operation.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>
                                System Stability:
                            </strong>
                            Helps prevent hardware and
                            software conflicts.
                        </li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 1.4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section1-4">
<h2>
                        1.4 Main Objectives of I/O Management
                    </h2>
<ul>
<li>
                            Provide a standard method
                            for accessing hardware.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Manage CPU and peripheral
                            communication.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Improve data transfer efficiency.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Control access to shared devices.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Detect and handle I/O errors.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Provide security for hardware
                            resources.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Make devices easier for
                            applications to use.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
