<!-- =====================================
                     PART 3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="part3">
<span class="part-heading">
                        Part 3
                    </span>
<h2>
                        How the Operating System
                        Manages Devices
                    </h2>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 3.1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-1">
<h2>
                        3.1 Role of the Operating System
                    </h2>
<p>
                        The operating system serves
                        as a communication link
                        between applications and hardware.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Applications normally request
                        services from the operating system
                        rather than directly controlling
                        hardware. The operating system
                        coordinates with device drivers
                        to complete those requests.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            A word processor sends a
                            print request to the operating
                            system. The operating system
                            coordinates with the printer
                            driver and printer.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 3.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-2">
<h2>
                        3.2 Device Drivers
                    </h2>
<p>
                        A device driver is specialized
                        software that allows the operating
                        system to communicate with a
                        particular hardware device.
                    </p>
<h3>Functions</h3>
<ol>
<li>
                            Hardware communication
                        </li>
<li>
                            Command translation
                        </li>
<li>
                            Device initialization
                        </li>
<li>
                            Data transfer coordination
                        </li>
<li>
                            Error reporting
                        </li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 3.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-3">
<h2>
                        3.3 Types of Device Drivers
                    </h2>
<h3>
                        Character Device Drivers
                    </h3>
<p>
                        Manage devices that transfer
                        data as streams of characters
                        or small units.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Examples: keyboards and serial ports.
                    </p>
<h3>
                        Block Device Drivers
                    </h3>
<p>
                        Manage devices that transfer
                        data in fixed-size blocks.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Examples: HDDs, SSDs, and
                        USB storage devices.
                    </p>
<h3>
                        Network Device Drivers
                    </h3>
<p>
                        Manage wired and wireless
                        network communication devices.
                    </p>
<h3>
                        Graphics Device Drivers
                    </h3>
<p>
                        Support communication between
                        the operating system and
                        graphics hardware.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 3.4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-4">
<h2>
                        3.4 Device Controllers
                    </h2>
<p>
                        A device controller is a hardware
                        component that manages the operation
                        of a specific device or group
                        of devices.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The operating system communicates
                        with controllers through suitable
                        device drivers.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            A storage controller coordinates
                            communication with storage devices
                            during read and write operations.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 3.5
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section3-5">
<h2>
                        3.5 Device Registers
                    </h2>
<p>
                        Device registers are small storage
                        locations in hardware controllers
                        that receive commands, report status,
                        and exchange information.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Data Register:</strong>
                            Holds transferred data.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Status Register:</strong>
                            Reports device conditions.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Control Register:</strong>
                            Receives device commands.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     PART 4
                     INPUT/OUTPUT TECHNIQUES
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="part4">
<span class="part-heading">
                        Part 4
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Input/Output Techniques
                    </h2>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 4.1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section4-1">
<h2>4.1 Programmed I/O</h2>
<p>
                        Programmed I/O is a technique in which the CPU
                        repeatedly checks a device's status and controls
                        the data transfer.
                    </p>
<p>
                        This repeated checking is called polling.
                    </p>
<h3>Process</h3>
<ol>
<li>The CPU sends a command.</li>
<li>The CPU checks device status.</li>
<li>The CPU waits for the device.</li>
<li>Data is transferred.</li>
<li>The operation completes.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Simple to understand</li>
<li>Easy to implement for basic devices</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>May waste CPU time</li>
<li>Less efficient for slow devices</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 4.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section4-2">
<h2>4.2 Interrupt-Driven I/O</h2>
<p>
                        Interrupt-driven I/O allows a device to notify the
                        CPU when attention is required or an operation
                        has been completed.
                    </p>
<h3>Process</h3>
<ol>
<li>The CPU sends a request.</li>
<li>The device performs the operation.</li>
<li>The CPU continues other work.</li>
<li>The device sends an interrupt.</li>
<li>The CPU handles the interrupt.</li>
<li>Normal execution continues.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reduces unnecessary CPU waiting</li>
<li>Supports efficient device communication</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>
                            Interrupt handling uses system resources.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Excessive interrupts can affect performance.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 4.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section4-3">
<h2>4.3 Direct Memory Access (DMA)</h2>
<p>
                        Direct Memory Access allows certain devices to
                        transfer information directly to or from main
                        memory with limited continuous CPU involvement.
                    </p>
<h3>How DMA Works</h3>
<ol>
<li>The CPU prepares transfer information.</li>
<li>
                            The CPU specifies memory locations and details.
                        </li>
<li>
                            The DMA controller manages the transfer.
                        </li>
<li>
                            Data moves between the device and memory.
                        </li>
<li>
                            The CPU is notified when necessary.
                        </li>
</ol>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reduces CPU involvement</li>
<li>Improves efficiency</li>
<li>Supports high-speed transfers</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Requires compatible hardware.</li>
<li>
                            Needs proper resource and memory management.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 4.4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section4-4">
<h2>4.4 Comparison of I/O Techniques</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Technique</th>
<th>How It Works</th>
<th>CPU Involvement</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Programmed I/O</td>
<td>
                                        CPU repeatedly checks device status
                                    </td>
<td>High</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Interrupt-Driven I/O</td>
<td>Device notifies CPU</td>
<td>Moderate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>DMA</td>
<td>
                                        Controller manages much of the transfer
                                    </td>
<td>Lower during transfer</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 4.5
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section4-5">
<h2>4.5 Synchronous and Asynchronous I/O</h2>
<h3>Synchronous I/O</h3>
<p>
                        The requesting process waits until the I/O operation
                        is completed before continuing past that operation.
                    </p>
<h3>Asynchronous I/O</h3>
<p>
                        The application can continue performing other work
                        while the I/O operation is being processed.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            An application that downloads data while remaining
                            responsive to the user demonstrates asynchronous I/O.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     PART 5
                     I/O MANAGEMENT OPERATIONS
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="part5">
<span class="part-heading">
                        Part 5
                    </span>
<h2>
                        I/O Management Techniques and Operations
                    </h2>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 5.1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section5-1">
<h2>5.1 Buffering</h2>
<p>
                        Buffering temporarily stores data in a memory area
                        called a buffer during transfer between devices
                        or applications.
                    </p>
<h3>Types of Buffering</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Single Buffering:</strong>
                            Uses one buffer.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Double Buffering:</strong>
                            Uses two buffers.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Circular Buffering:</strong>
                            Reuses a fixed-size buffer continuously.
                        </li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            Online video players temporarily store data
                            in a buffer to help maintain smooth playback.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 5.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section5-2">
<h2>5.2 Caching</h2>
<p>
                        Caching stores frequently accessed information
                        in a faster storage area so it can be retrieved
                        more quickly later.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Improves access speed</li>
<li>Reduces repeated device operations</li>
<li>Improves application responsiveness</li>
<li>Reduces unnecessary storage activity</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 5.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section5-3">
<h2>5.3 Spooling</h2>
<p>
                        Spooling stands for Simultaneous Peripheral
                        Operations On-Line.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It places I/O tasks into a temporary queue
                        so devices can process them in an organized manner.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            When five students send documents to one printer,
                            the operating system places the print jobs
                            in a queue.
                        </p>
</div>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Organizes multiple requests</li>
<li>
                            Allows applications to continue working
                        </li>
<li>
                            Reduces conflicts over shared devices
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 5.4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section5-4">
<h2>5.4 Device Scheduling</h2>
<p>
                        Device scheduling determines the order in which
                        multiple I/O requests are processed.
                    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>FCFS</td>
<td>
                                        Processes requests in arrival order.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Priority Scheduling</td>
<td>
                                        Processes requests according to priority.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SSTF</td>
<td>
                                        Chooses the shortest disk-head movement.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SCAN</td>
<td>
                                        Services disk requests while moving
                                        in one direction, then reverses.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>
                        SSTF and SCAN are especially relevant to
                        traditional hard disk drives.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 5.5
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section5-5">
<h2>5.5 Error Handling in I/O Management</h2>
<p>
                        Error handling identifies and responds to problems
                        during communication between devices and the
                        operating system.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li>Device Not Found</li>
<li>Device Not Ready</li>
<li>Read Error</li>
<li>Write Error</li>
<li>Driver Error</li>
<li>Connection Error</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            Disconnecting a USB drive while saving a file
                            may cause a write error because the transfer
                            cannot finish successfully.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     PART 6
                     HARDWARE INTERFACES
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="part6">
<span class="part-heading">
                        Part 6
                    </span>
<h2>
                        Device Communication and Hardware Interfaces
                    </h2>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 6.1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section6-1">
<h2>6.1 Device Communication</h2>
<p>
                        Device communication refers to the exchange of
                        information between a computer and its hardware.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Operating systems coordinate this communication
                        using drivers, controllers, system buses,
                        and hardware interfaces.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 6.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section6-2">
<h2>6.2 System Bus</h2>
<p>
                        A system bus is a communication pathway connecting
                        components such as the CPU, memory, and I/O controllers.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Data Bus:</strong>
                            Transfers data.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Address Bus:</strong>
                            Identifies read and write locations.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Control Bus:</strong>
                            Carries control signals.
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 6.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section6-3">
<h2>6.3 USB Interface</h2>
<p>
                        Universal Serial Bus is an interface used to connect
                        external hardware devices to computers.
                    </p>
<p>
                        USB supports data transfer and, depending on the
                        connection, electrical power delivery.
                    </p>
<h3>Common USB Devices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keyboards</li>
<li>Mice</li>
<li>Flash drives</li>
<li>Printers</li>
<li>External storage</li>
<li>Webcams</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
