                    </h2>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 7.1
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section7-1">
<h2>7.1 What Is an Interrupt?</h2>
<p>
                        An interrupt is a signal or event that causes
                        the CPU to temporarily change its execution flow
                        so an important event can be handled.
                    </p>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
                            A keyboard may generate an interrupt when
                            a key is pressed.
                        </p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 7.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section7-2">
<h2>7.2 Types of Interrupts</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Hardware Interrupt:</strong>
                            Generated by hardware devices.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Software Interrupt:</strong>
                            Generated by software or processor instructions.
                        </li>
<li>
<strong>Timer Interrupt:</strong>
                            Supports time-related operating system activities.
                        </li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 7.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section7-3">
<h2>7.3 Interrupt Service Routine (ISR)</h2>
<p>
                        An Interrupt Service Routine is a special function
                        executed in response to an interrupt.
                    </p>
<p>
                        It handles or acknowledges the event and performs
                        necessary immediate processing.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 7.4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section7-4">
<h2>7.4 Interrupt Priority</h2>
<p>
                        Interrupt priority determines which interrupt
                        should receive attention first when several
                        interrupt requests occur.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 7.5
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section7-5">
<h2>7.5 Device Resource Allocation</h2>
<p>
                        Device resource allocation assigns hardware
                        resources required by connected devices.
                    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Resource</th>
<th>Function</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>I/O Ports</td>
<td>
                                        Provide communication access to hardware.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>IRQs</td>
<td>
                                        Allow devices to notify the processor.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>DMA Channels</td>
<td>
                                        Support direct data transfers.
                                    </td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory Addresses</td>
<td>
                                        Identify hardware or memory locations.
                                    </td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
