<section class="chapter" id="section8-1">
<h2>8.1 Device Management in Windows</h2>
<p>
                        Microsoft Windows provides tools to detect,
                        configure, update, and troubleshoot hardware.
                    </p>
<h3>Windows Device Manager</h3>
<ul>
<li>Displays connected hardware.</li>
<li>Shows device status.</li>
<li>Manages device drivers.</li>
<li>Allows driver updates and uninstallation.</li>
<li>Helps troubleshoot device problems.</li>
<li>Supports Plug and Play devices.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 8.2
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section8-2">
<h2>8.2 Device Management in Linux</h2>
<p>
                        Linux uses kernel components and device drivers
                        to communicate with hardware.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Many devices are represented by special files
                        in the /dev directory.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li>
<code>/dev/sda</code> –
                            Possible storage device name
                        </li>
<li>
<code>/dev/tty</code> –
                            Terminal-related device
                        </li>
<li>
<code>/dev/null</code> –
                            Discards written data
                        </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 8.3
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section8-3">
<h2>8.3 Device Management in macOS</h2>
<p>
                        macOS manages devices through operating system
                        components and driver frameworks.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Apple's I/O Kit provides an object-oriented
                        framework for hardware communication and
                        device driver management.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 8.4
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section8-4">
<h2>8.4 Plug and Play (PnP)</h2>
<p>
                        Plug and Play allows an operating system to
                        automatically detect and configure supported
                        hardware devices.
                    </p>
<h3>Process</h3>
<ol>
<li>A device is connected.</li>
<li>The operating system detects it.</li>
<li>The device is identified.</li>
<li>A suitable driver is located or installed.</li>
<li>Required resources are assigned.</li>
<li>The device becomes available.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- =====================================
                     SECTION 8.5
                ====================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="section8-5">
<h2>8.5 Hot Plugging</h2>
<p>
                        Hot plugging allows supported devices to be
                        connected or removed while a computer is running.
                    </p>
<h3>Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Connecting a USB keyboard</li>
<li>Connecting an external monitor</li>
<li>Attaching external storage</li>
</ul>
<p>
